@page "/focus-order"
<PageTitle>Focus order · Blazor Accessibility Toolkit</PageTitle>

<div class="page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Browser tests · Playwright</p>
    <h1>Focus order</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        <code>CaptureFocusOrderAsync</code> presses Tab until focus leaves the page, wraps around, cycles or gets stuck,
        and records every stop with its role and accessible name. You compare it with the order you expect, and
        heuristics flag keyboard traps, stuck focus, focus on invisible elements and focus without a visible indicator.
    </p>
</div>

<RunAllTests />

<div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data">
        <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Reported when</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>blazor-focus-hidden-target</code></td><td>focus lands on an element that is invisible or hidden from assistive technologies</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>blazor-focus-trap</code></td><td>focus cycles within part of the page that is not a modal dialog</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>blazor-focus-stuck</code></td><td>Tab does not move focus</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>blazor-focus-repeated</code></td><td>one element is reached twice in a single pass</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>blazor-focus-indicator-not-detected</code></td><td>the focused element draws neither an outline nor a box shadow (opt-in: <code>ReportMissingFocusIndicator</code>)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    These are potential issues: the toolkit records what happened and flags patterns, but whether an order is
    <em>logical</em> is decided by the expectation you write with <code>ShouldMatch</code>.
</p>

<section aria-labelledby="collapsed">
    <h2 id="collapsed">A panel that only looks collapsed</h2>
    <p>
        Animating a panel closed with <code>opacity: 0</code> and <code>max-height: 0</code> hides it from sight but not
        from the keyboard. Collapse both panels below, then press Tab from the Filters button: in the first one, focus
        disappears into three invisible controls before it reaches "Show 24 results".
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="A filter panel collapsed with CSS only" Defect="true" Example="FocusOrderExamples.AnimatedFilterOrder" Sources="@(["AnimatedFilterPanel.razor"])"
                 Affects="@(["Keyboard users", "Screen magnifier users"])">
        <Stage><AnimatedFilterPanel /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
    <ExampleCard Title="A filter panel collapsed with hidden" Example="FocusOrderExamples.FilterOrder" Sources="@(["FilterPanel.razor"])">
        <Stage><FilterPanel /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="indicator">
    <h2 id="indicator">Where is focus?</h2>
    <p>
        A designer removes the focus ring because it "looks ugly". Mouse users never notice; keyboard users lose track
        of where they are. Both switchers below have the same markup, so a markup audit passes both. Click the text
        above each one and press Tab: in the first, nothing shows which button has focus.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="Toggle buttons without a focus indicator" Defect="true" Example="FocusOrderExamples.BareFocusIndicator" Sources="@(["OutlineFreeViewSwitcher.razor"])"
                 Affects="@(["Keyboard users", "Low vision users"])">
        <Stage><OutlineFreeViewSwitcher /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
    <ExampleCard Title="Toggle buttons with a visible focus outline" Example="FocusOrderExamples.VisibleFocusIndicator" Sources="@(["ViewSwitcher.razor"])"
                 Hears="List, toggle button, pressed">
        <Stage><ViewSwitcher /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
    <p>
        The check looks for an outline or a box shadow, the usual indicators. An indicator made another way, such as a
        change of background, is reported as well, as a potential issue: a person decides whether it is visible enough.
    </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="modal">
    <h2 id="modal">A cycle that is not a trap</h2>
    <p>
        Inside an open modal dialog, Tab is supposed to cycle. The capture sees that the stops are inside an
        <code>aria-modal="true"</code> dialog (or one opened with <code>showModal()</code>) and does not report a trap.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="Focus order inside an open modal dialog" Example="FocusOrderExamples.DialogFocusCycle">
        <Stage>
            <ModalDialog TriggerText="Delete account" Title="Delete account?" ConfirmText="Delete">
                <p>This removes your profile and cannot be undone.</p>
            </ModalDialog>
        </Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

